#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8559e2bf-8b48-5e26-81c6-0db03d93645a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5451" :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/6191f617d2c2e89e07c1cc349a5926874f11ea78d046185ce990e566225a7c79.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/id #uuid "ef17b637-7c4d-5515-99b7-13b1290244f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5452" :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)"}]}
